# DTF tier manifest: scan-queue-upload-image-rescan
#   (artifact-keeper#5946, PR #5950; #5947, PR #5954; main only, for 1.12.0)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. LOCAL-ONLY: scans run the backend image's
# bundled Grype with its pre-seeded DB; no scanner sidecar, no network beyond
# pulling busybox:1.37.0-musl (pinned by digest) for the #5942 grype stub. The
# oracle recreates the backend (same postgres and data volume) for each phase
# with fixtures/scan-queue.yml, and for the rescan phase also
# fixtures/rescan-cap.yml.
#
# THE CHANGES
# #5946  With SCAN_QUEUE_ENABLED=true, scan_on_upload queues its scan on the
#        durable scan queue instead of spawning it in the process that took
#        the upload: a pushed OCI image is one `hosted_image` job per
#        (repository, manifest digest), so pushing the same manifest again (or
#        under another tag) adds no job; every other upload is a
#        `hosted_upload` job per artifact. A worker runs them.
# #5947  With SCAN_RESCAN_MAX_PER_DAY > 0, a worker compares each scan type's
#        current vulnerability-database build (what the scanner reports live,
#        and what recent scans recorded in scan_results.vuln_db_published_at)
#        with the build each stored artifact's newest scan used. Every digest
#        graded on an older build is queued ONCE as a `rescan` job at priority
#        5, below hosted scans (10). Earlier scan_results rows are kept.
#
# HOW THE DB BUILD CHANGE IS SIMULATED: the oracle seeds the recorded build on
# the existing Grype scan rows of two digests to 2020-01-01 (UPDATE scan_results SET
# vuln_db_published_at), so the bundled Grype DB is "newer" than what those
# rows were graded on. A third digest keeps its real build and must not be
# queued. A rescan job is claimed by the worker within a second of being
# queued, so the rescan phase installs a test-only trigger on scan_queue that
# pushes a new `rescan` row's next_attempt_at an hour out (the enqueue itself
# is untouched); the oracle reads the jobs through the admin API, then drops
# the trigger and makes them due.
#
# THE GATES
#   (FIX)      RED on ghcr main (no hosted_upload / hosted_image / rescan
#              kinds: scan_on_upload scans in-process, SCAN_RESCAN_MAX_PER_DAY
#              is unknown). GREEN on main + #5954 (which contains #5950).
#              #5946 queue on, worker off: an image pushed three times (tag
#                    v1, v1 again, v2) and a generic upload are one
#                    hosted_image job (the repository and the manifest digest)
#                    and one hosted_upload job per upload in
#                    GET /api/v1/admin/security/scan-queue, and nothing is
#                    scanned before a worker runs;
#              #5946 worker on: the queue drains and every tag row and upload
#                    gets a completed scan written after the worker started;
#              #5947 SCAN_RESCAN_MAX_PER_DAY=100: exactly one `rescan` job per
#                    seeded digest (one generic digest stored in two
#                    repositories, one image digest under two tags), priority
#                    below the hosted jobs, the unseeded digest not queued; once
#                    due, each row of those digests gets a new completed scan
#                    on a newer build and the seeded rows are still there.
#   (CONTROL)  must hold on BOTH images:
#              queue off (the default): a generic upload and an image push
#              into scan_on_upload repositories are scanned in-process and
#              leave no job in the queue view, which reports enabled=false;
#              queue on and SCAN_RESCAN_MAX_PER_DAY unset: the seeded rows
#              queue no rescan job and get no new scan in 30 s.
#
# #5942 (artifact-keeper, for 1.12.3): a scanner that never returns.
#   fixtures/grype-stub.yml puts fixtures/grype-stub in front of the bundled
#   grype (a static busybox copied out of busybox:1.37.0-musl is its
#   interpreter; the backend image has no shell): DTF_GRYPE_STUB_DELAY=hang
#   never returns, a number delays the real grype that many seconds. The
#   backend runs with SCANNER_PROCESS_TIMEOUT_SECS=30, the knob #5942 adds
#   (unset by default: no limit of its own). Seconds, not production minutes:
#   the gate is the relationship between the delay and the limit.
#   (FIX)      RED on ghcr main before the fix (the knob is unknown: the hung
#              grype's row stays `running`, the queued job holds its 600 s
#              budget). GREEN on the fix.
#              queue off: an on-demand scan whose grype never returns has its
#                    grype row `failed` 30 to 55 s after the trigger, the error
#                    naming SCANNER_PROCESS_TIMEOUT_SECS=30s, the dependency row
#                    completed and no hung grype process left in the backend;
#              queue and worker on: the same scan's grype row is `failed` at
#                    the limit and its hosted_artifact job goes back to the
#                    queue with the timeout as last_error, then runs a second
#                    attempt (attempts >= 2), instead of completing.
#   (CONTROL)  both images: a grype that takes 6 s, under the 30 s limit,
#              completes, and completes no sooner than 6 s after the trigger
#              (so the stub really sat in front of grype).
#
# #6282 (artifact-keeper, for 1.12.3): a retry that reuses an identical scan.
#   (FIX)      RED on main with #6266 and without its fix (the dedup
#              conversion only converts a `running` row, so the timed-out row
#              stays `failed` while the job completes). GREEN on the fix.
#              Phase 8, same backend as phase 7: after a queued on-demand
#              scan's grype attempt times out, the same bytes stored as another
#              artifact (repository sq-twin) get a completed grype scan row (the
#              oracle writes it, since the stub hangs every real grype run). The
#              retry reuses it: the timed-out row ends `completed`, is_reused,
#              source_scan_id that row, and holds that scan's finding only.
#
# #6232 (artifact-keeper, for 1.13.0): a replica that dies mid-scan.
#   (CONTROL)  both images: queue and worker on, the grype stub hangs (no
#              process limit in reach): a hosted_upload job's grype row stays
#              `running` and its claim keeps its token across a 35 s window,
#              longer than the 30 s recovery sweep.
#   (FIX)      RED on main (the row stays `running` until the stuck-scan
#              janitor fails it 1800 s after it started). GREEN on the fix.
#              The oracle kills the backend (SIGKILL) mid-scan, ages the dead
#              claim past its TTL instead of waiting 120 s, and recreates the
#              backend: its first recovery sweep requeues the job (attempts 1)
#              and, within 60 s, fails the dead claim's grype row with an
#              error naming the lapsed claim.
#
# #6423 (artifact-keeper, for 1.13.0): a slow worker whose claim lapses on the
#   job's last attempt.
#   (CONTROL)  both images: queue and worker on, the grype stub takes 75 s.
#              The oracle ages the live claim and spends all but the job's
#              last attempt; the next sweep revokes the claim and fails its
#              grype row with the recovery reason.
#   (FIX)      RED on main after #6239 (recovery ends the job `failed`, the
#              claim guard refuses the slow worker's late verdict, and nothing
#              reruns the job: the artifact keeps no grype verdict). GREEN on
#              the fix: recovery runs the job once more (the oracle skips its
#              120 s backoff), that run's grype row completes within 165 s, the
#              slow worker's row stays as recovery failed it, and the job is
#              retired.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: ~200 API calls back to back.
COVERS="5946 5947 5942 6282 6232 6423"
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
